# High-contrast black and white through a "red filter". A black-and-white conversion is a choice of how much each
# colour channel counts: a red filter on the lens (or here, the channel mixer) makes red and skin light, and blue sky
# and foliage dark, so clouds stand out and faces glow. Desaturating instead would weigh the channels by luma and
# give a flat grey picture.
#   red_filter    rgb_mixer with the same row for red, green and blue: one monochrome channel made from mostly red,
#                 some green and a little negative blue (the row sums to 1, so neutral greys keep their level)
#   print         a hard S curve: deep blacks, bright whites, the separation of a graded fibre print
#   clarity       wide local contrast for the "dodged and burned" look of a darkroom print
#   sky_burn      the top of the frame burned in, as a printer would under the enlarger
#   grain         monochrome grain, a fast stock look
#   edges         a soft vignette
# Use it for portraits, street, landscapes with dramatic skies; swap the mixer row for other filters (green filter:
# [0.2, 0.9, -0.1] lightens foliage; blue filter darkens skin and lightens sky).
cierra 0.1
name "black and white, red filter"
about "Monochrome from mostly red: dark skies, bright skin; a hard print curve, darkroom clarity, grain."
input aces2065_1
output aces2(display: rec1886)

node red_filter = rgb_mixer(red: [1, 0.2, -0.2], green: [1, 0.2, -0.2], blue: [1, 0.2, -0.2])
  label "monochrome from mostly red"
node print = curve(points: [[0.0729, 0.0729], [0.22, 0.18], [0.4136, 0.42], [0.6, 0.66], [0.9, 0.95], [1.2, 1.1]])
  label "hard print S"
node clarity = local_contrast(amount: 0.3, radius_px: 120)
node sky_burn = exposure(stops: -0.5)
  label "burn the sky in"
  mask graduated(centre: [0.5, 0.35], angle: 90, falloff: [0.15, 0.3])
node film_grain = grain(amount: 0.22, size_px: 1.6, chroma: 0, seed: 400)
node edges = vignette(amount: 0.2, softness: 0.8)

graph red_filter -> print -> clarity -> sky_burn -> film_grain -> edges
